{
  "comment": "The vectors binding cyfr-keeper (apps/keeper, Go) and its clients, Locus's builds and backends services (apps/locus/lib/locus/keeper.ex, Elixir) and the Opus worker service (apps/opus/lib/opus/keeper/channel.ex, Elixir), the Elixir clients through one codec (apps/prima/lib/prima/keeper_protocol.ex): the keeper accepts every valid request and refuses every invalid one with its code, every side encodes each frame to the bytes given, and the clients understand every reply. `reserved_env_prefixes` are the variable name prefixes no spawn may carry in its `env`, CYFR's, Locus's and the keeper's own (its channel variable is KEEPER_CHANNEL): the keeper refuses them, and CYFR and the backends service refuse a backend definition that names one. `frames` are the stdio and attach streams every client carries; `control_frames` are the control stream (4) of a spawn that asked for a control channel with `control: true`, carrying Prima.RunnerControl lines (tests/fixtures/runner_control.json) verbatim between a worker service and its runner's fd 3, and a zero-length one ending the stream. A client that never asks for the channel refuses a control frame. `relay_frames` are the relay stream (5) of a spawn of an isolated pool, carrying Prima.RunnerRelay frames (tests/fixtures/runner_relay.json) verbatim between a worker service and its runner's fd 4: the keeper sends nothing on it, its end frame included, until the client has sent on it first, and the client's first frame opens it, a zero-length one included, which ends nothing; after that a zero-length frame ends it. `relay_streams` pin that rule on the relay: a spawn whose client never opens the stream receives no stream-5 frame, and one whose client opens it receives what the command wrote to fd 4 and the stream's end. A spawn of any pool may carry `memory_bytes`, its memory bound, from 16777216 to 1099511627776: zero is not 'no bound', and a bound that is not a whole number of bytes in that range is refused. Every `exited` says whether the kernel killed processes of the spawn at that bound (`memory_exceeded`), and a keeper that cannot enforce a bound refuses the spawn as `memory_unavailable`. A spawn's `isolation` is `netns` or absent: the keeper names its isolated pools on its command line (`--pool runner:30101-30108:netns`), starts every spawn of one in a user and network namespace of its own that maps the pooled uid and gid to themselves, and refuses as `bad_request` a spawn of an isolated pool without `isolation` and one of any other pool with it, a decision of the pool it names and so not a vector here; the runner spawns carry it, and Locus's build and backends spawns, whose pools are not isolated, do not.",
